Z||000011||fiero56||04-27-2000||11:40 PM||||Check radiator cap too. Supposed to be 16lb release, and non-vented. I'm serious, I had a bad cap and it would allow the engine to overheat.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Frank R||04-28-2000||01:46 AM||||Cooling systems do not usually overheat because of the absence of the thermostat.  If the thermostat is removed and the cap is removed from housing, water will not overheat, just loose water.  The job of a thermostat is to maintain a predetermined operating temperature, as 180-215 Degrees F.<BR>The job of a pressure cap is to raise the boiling temperature.  Water will boil at 212 degrees F at sea level.  A 16 pound pressure cap will raise the boiling point of water 2 degrees for every pound of cap pressure.  Hence the boiling point of the system will be 212 plus 32 equals 244 degrees.  This is one way the mfgr can use a small radiator to keep the temps manageable.  Small radiators cost less to make.    Frank R||||reg||6
Z||000013||RossT||04-28-2000||03:07 AM||||Wayne, do a search for "water pump".  There has been some talk about plastic impellers on replacement waterpumps, not moving the antifreeze. thus the overflow out the overflow tank.  The solution was to replace the waterpump again, with a metal impeller.||||reg||1
